+
+# Scope and results
+
+This local experiment extends the stage-3 forward-only Residual Add RMSNorm work with an actual two-kernel Triton backward. The row kernel computes both input gradients and FP32 partial weight gradients; a second kernel reduces partials across rows. Both the normalized output and observable residual sum are part of the operator contract, so the row kernel includes `grad_residual_out` when supplied. It uses `1.0 / tl.sqrt` because this vendor Triton build lacks `tl.rsqrt`; it does not require FP16/BF16 atomic add.
+
+Three independent processes with seeds 13001–13003 tested FP16 and BF16, `grad_residual_out` present and absent, and six shapes: 64×768, 64×1024, 64×4096, 7×769, 16×4099, and unseen tail 17×1537. All 72 cases passed comparisons of `grad_x`, `grad_residual`, and `grad_weight` against both the stage-3 analytical PyTorch formula and independent PyTorch autograd at `atol=rtol=0.03`. In every case, input tensors were unchanged and `grad_x` equaled `grad_residual` bitwise. The manifest links raw per-sample timings, error maxima, script hash, and file hashes.
+
+Backward-only warm GPU-event timings used five warmups, nine samples of 20 launches, and alternating candidate/baseline sample order. The three-run geometric-mean speedups across the 24 distinct cases were 10.379–15.405× versus the **analytical PyTorch backward sequence**. Each individual round was faster. This comparison includes the PyTorch sequence's many launches and temporary tensors; it is not a comparison against another optimized fused backward, does not include forward or optimizer work, and is not a full-training throughput claim.
+
+The runnable source is [`stage13_backward.py`](../../evidence/bi-v150-corex42-stage13/stage13_backward.py). The evidence is local to BI-V150, CoreX 4.2.0, and the recorded shapes and dtypes; it does not establish NVIDIA instruction equivalence or performance portability.
