+
+# Scope and results
+
+This local experiment joins the stage-3 forward kernel and stage-13 two-kernel backward through a `torch.autograd.Function` with both normalized and observable residual outputs. A synthetic loss consumes both outputs and performs one SGD weight update. A separate PyTorch implementation provides the reference. Three independent processes (seeds 14001–14003) tested FP16/BF16 and 3D shapes `[8, 2, 1024]`, `[7, 1, 769]`, and `[4, 4, 4099]`: all 18 case-rounds passed output, residual, loss, both input gradients, weight gradient, and updated-weight checks. Across them, maximum absolute errors were 0.000977 for output, 0 for residual, 3.58e-7 for loss, 3.05e-5 for either input gradient, 5.96e-8 for weight gradient, and 0 for updated weight. The [manifest](../../evidence/bi-v150-corex42-stage14/manifest.json) and round JSONs retain the exact values and hashes; the [standalone script](../../evidence/bi-v150-corex42-stage14/stage14_autograd_integration.py) has both imported kernel scripts beside it.
+
+This proves a small synthetic forward/backward/update path for the recorded shapes. It contains no Megatron model step, performance timing, training-throughput claim, or real workload shape capture.
+
+The pinned Megatron checkout was also probed. Import first resolved the CoreX distribution's ordinary `megatron` package instead of the pinned namespace checkout and failed to find `megatron.core.extensions`. A temporary import overlay selected the pinned package, then import stopped because the available `transformer_engine` stub lacked `__version__`. The checkout and vendor package were not modified. The [route JSON](../../evidence/bi-v150-corex42-stage14/megatron-route-probe-final.json) and [overlay traceback](../../evidence/bi-v150-corex42-stage14/megatron-import-overlay.log) preserve these distinct failures. Real Megatron integration remains unverified until a compatible runtime is available.
