+# What ran
+
+The pinned Megatron-Core local GPT layer spec selected its PyTorch RMSNorm path
+after a process-local mask for an incompatible Transformer Engine stub and a
+process-local mapping of CoreX PyTorch 2.4's older DTensor export path. Neither
+the vendor package nor the pinned Megatron checkout was modified. A one-layer,
+single-process GPT model completed forward, backward, and SGD update with
+synthetic token IDs. Hooks observed three contiguous FP32 RMSNorm inputs of
+shape `[8, 2, 256]`, strides `[512, 256, 1]`. This is an actual model call
+shape for this small test configuration, not a production workload capture.
+
+The fused candidate replaces exactly one adjacent pair: attention
+bias/dropout/add (dropout zero) followed by pre-MLP RMSNorm. The attention
+output bias, when present, is added before the fused operation. The candidate
+uses the stage-3 forward and stage-13 two-kernel backward through a
+`torch.autograd.Function`, preserving the observable residual sum. Other GPT
+operations remain Megatron local implementations.
+
+Three independent processes, seeds 21005–21007, each ran one reference and
+candidate correctness step, three warmups, and 12 alternated-order timed step
+pairs. All passed relative and absolute `torch.allclose` checks for per-token
+loss and the post-SGD parameter threshold. Maximum output absolute error was
+0.0008483 across the three runs; maximum updated-parameter absolute error was
+6.615e-6. The three reference/candidate median full-step times were
+3.7784/3.6944 ms, 3.7747/3.6905 ms, and 3.7748/3.7071 ms, respectively:
+ratios 1.0227, 1.0228, and 1.0183. The 17-token held-out sequence also passed
+and measured 3.8171/3.7328 ms (1.0226 ratio). Each step processed two
+sequences on one BI-V150; all timings use synchronized GPU events.
+
+An additional three-process test placed the same model step under PyTorch BF16
+autocast (seeds 22002–22004; three warmups and 12 alternated timed pairs per
+process). All output, loss, and post-SGD parameter checks passed. However, the
+captured candidate inputs, residuals, weight, and normalized output were **all
+FP32**, despite the surrounding BF16 autocast context. Reference/candidate
+step medians were 4.7095/5.1258, 4.7275/5.0951, and 4.6780/5.0646 ms:
+ratios 0.9188, 0.9279, and 0.9237. This is a reproducible slowdown for this
+autocast context and is **not** a BF16 fused-kernel result. Avoid selecting the
+candidate here based only on the FP32 no-autocast result.
+
+All ratios apply only to the tested single-layer local fallback with synthetic
+tokens. They do not establish a production model speedup, FP16/BF16 fused model
+behavior, Transformer Engine behavior, or a general Hopper/Blackwell
+instruction equivalent. The full JSON samples, script hashes, and environment
+are in the bundle manifest. Earlier exploratory failures and their JSONs
+remain in the downstream experiment repository.
