+# Residual Add RMSNorm backward on BI-V150
+
+The stage-3 forward produces normalized output `y` and an observable residual sum `r = x + residual`. With incoming gradients `g = grad_y` and optional `h = grad_r_observed`, define `inv = (mean(r²) + epsilon)^(-1/2)` and `dot = sum(g * weight * r)` per row. The shared input gradient is
+
+```text
+grad_x = grad_residual = g * weight * inv - r * inv^3 * dot / hidden + h
+grad_weight[j] = sum_rows(g[:, j] * r[:, j] * inv[:])
+```
+
+Omit `h` when the observable residual output has no incoming gradient. The first Triton kernel computes each row, stores both input gradients, and writes FP32 partial weight gradients. The second kernel reduces those partials across rows. Its central row calculation is:
+
+```python
+inv = 1.0 / tl.sqrt(tl.sum(r * r, axis=0) / HIDDEN + EPS)
+weighted = dy * w
+dot = tl.sum(weighted * r, axis=0)
+dr = weighted * inv - r * ((inv * inv * inv) / HIDDEN) * dot
+if HAS_H:
+    dr = dr + tl.load(H + offset, mask=valid, other=0).to(tl.float32)
+tl.store(DX + offset, dr, mask=valid)
+tl.store(DRES + offset, dr, mask=valid)
+tl.store(PARTIAL + offset, dy * r * inv, mask=valid)
+```
+
+The [runnable source](../../evidence/bi-v150-corex42-stage13/stage13_backward.py) includes masked tail handling, launches, independent autograd comparison, and the exact timing sequence. FP16 and BF16 passed all 72 case-round combinations over six shapes and two observable-gradient modes. Each input gradient matched its counterpart bitwise; every case passed both analytical and autograd checks at `atol=rtol=0.03`. The evidence [manifest](../../evidence/bi-v150-corex42-stage13/manifest.json) lists every result and SHA256 receipt.
+
+This is a backward-only microbenchmark against a multi-op analytical PyTorch sequence. Its 10.379–15.405× range includes launch and temporary-tensor savings in that baseline. It does not demonstrate full-training throughput, an optimized backward comparison, or NVIDIA-specific instruction behavior. Keep the previous [forward page](residual-rmsnorm-bi-v150.md) scoped to forward measurements.
+
+The separate [synthetic autograd integration](../../sources/experiments/bi-v150-corex42-stage14.md) connected this backward to the stage-3 forward and one SGD weight update. All 18 FP16/BF16 case-rounds passed comparison with PyTorch, including both observable outputs and all three gradients. Subsequently, a [pinned Megatron GPT local-route test](../../sources/experiments/bi-v150-corex42-stage21.md) used process-local compatibility mappings to run one complete one-layer FP32 model step and fused a single attention BDA-to-pre-MLP RMSNorm pair. Three independent processes passed the model output, loss, and post-SGD parameter checks, with 1.0183–1.0228 full-step median ratios on synthetic tokens. A BF16-autocast context still fed FP32 tensors to this fusion point and slowed the whole step to 0.9188–0.9279 of the local baseline in three repeats. The installed Transformer Engine remains incompatible; these figures do not cover its optimized route, a production model, or FP16/BF16 fused full-model training.
+
+## Native Megatron loop, 2026-10-02
+
