+# Two-pass scan on BI-V150
+
+The tested FP32 prototype first writes per-chunk sums, then computes a local `tl.cumsum` and adds preceding chunk sums. It passed against `torch.cumsum` for 4099 and 65537 elements in three independent runs, including masked tails. It was slower than the framework baseline on both shapes, so it is **correctness evidence and an optimization starting point**, not a performance recommendation or a port of CCCL internals.
+
+The core of the [second pass](../../evidence/bi-v150-corex42-migration-batch1/stage6_exp_scan.py) is:
+
+```python
+previous = tl.load(SUMS + chunk_ids, mask=chunk_ids < chunk, other=0)
+carry = tl.sum(previous, 0)
+result = tl.cumsum(values, 0) + carry
+tl.store(Y + offsets, result, mask=offsets < N)
+```
+
+The complete source includes the first pass, masks, reference comparison, and event samples. The second pass re-reads preceding chunk sums, which can become expensive as the chunk count grows. The [source record](../../sources/experiments/bi-v150-corex42-migration-batch1.md) retains raw timings and failure records.
